from common import S,X
ECO="A community of living organisms together with the non living things around them::community,living,organisms,organism|They interact with one another in a particular area::interact,interacting,area,place,surroundings"
CATS="Terrestrial (on land), e.g. forest or farm::terrestrial,land,forest,farm,savanna|Aquatic (in water), e.g. pond or river::aquatic,water,pond,river,lake|Arboreal (on trees), e.g. a tall forest tree::arboreal,tree,trees"
DEC="Decomposers such as bacteria and fungi break down dead organisms and waste::decompos,bacteria,fungi,break down,dead|They return nutrients to the soil or water::nutrients,return,soil"
QA=[
S("Define an ecosystem",2,"An ecosystem is a community of living organisms together with the non living things in their surroundings, all interacting with one another in a particular area.",ECO),
S("Define the term ecosystem",2,"An ecosystem is a community of living organisms together with the non living things in their surroundings, all interacting in a particular area.",ECO),
S("Explain why an ecosystem is described as self sustaining",3,"It provides most of what its organisms need: plants capture sunlight to make food, animals eat plants or other animals, and decomposers return nutrients to the soil or water. Only energy from the Sun must come from outside.",
  "Plants capture energy from sunlight to make food::plants,producers,sunlight,photosynthesis,food|Animals feed on plants or other animals::animals,eat,consumers|Decomposers return nutrients so they are used again::decompos,nutrients,return,recycle,cycle"),
S("Name the three categories of ecosystems and give one example",3,"Terrestrial (on land, e.g. a forest), aquatic (in water, e.g. a pond) and arboreal (on trees, e.g. a mango tree).",CATS),
S("Name the three categories of ecosystems",3,"Terrestrial, aquatic and arboreal.","Terrestrial::terrestrial|Aquatic::aquatic|Arboreal::arboreal"),
S("State one way in which a pond ecosystem differs from a forest ecosystem",2,"A pond is an aquatic ecosystem found in water while a forest is a terrestrial ecosystem found on land; in a pond tiny floating plants are the base of the food chain.",
  "A pond is found in water (aquatic) while a forest is found on land (terrestrial)::water,land,aquatic,terrestrial"),
S("List two biotic components and two abiotic components of a pond",4,"Biotic: algae, water plants, tilapia, tadpoles, frogs. Abiotic: water, dissolved oxygen, sunlight, temperature, mud.",
  "A biotic component such as algae, fish or frogs::algae,plants,fish,tilapia,catfish,frog,tadpole,kingfisher,insects,bacteria|A second biotic component::algae,plants,fish,tilapia,catfish,frog,tadpole,kingfisher,insects,bacteria|An abiotic component such as water or sunlight::water,sunlight,oxygen,temperature,mud,light|A second abiotic component::water,sunlight,oxygen,temperature,mud,light"),
S("State one way in which organisms of an arboreal ecosystem differ",2,"Arboreal organisms have strong limbs, gripping hands, claws and often a tail for climbing and balance, while aquatic organisms have gills, fins and a streamlined body for swimming.",
  "Arboreal organisms are adapted for climbing or gripping (limbs, claws, tails)::climb,climbing,grip,gripping,claws,limbs,tail,perch|Aquatic organisms are adapted for swimming (gills, fins)::gills,fins,swim,swimming,streamlined"),
S("Write a food chain for a terrestrial ecosystem",2,"Grass, then grasshopper, then toad, then snake, then eagle (the arrows show the direction of energy flow).",
  "It begins with a producer such as grass::grass,plant,producer,leaves|Followed by a consumer, then another consumer::grasshopper,toad,snake,eagle,goat,antelope,rat,lion|Arrows show the direction of energy::arrow,arrows,energy,eaten"),
S("Write a food chain that begins with algae",2,"Algae, then small fish, then catfish, then kingfisher.",
  "It begins with algae::algae|Followed by consumers such as small fish, catfish or kingfisher::fish,catfish,kingfisher,tadpole,tilapia"),
S("Explain how cutting down trees in a forest can affect the soil and the animals",3,"Animals such as monkeys and birds lose food and shelter; the soil is exposed to heat and erosion; and the number of decomposers falls.",
  "Animals lose food and shelter::food,shelter,home,habitat|The soil is exposed to heat and erosion::erosion,exposed,heat,washed|Decomposers or the amount of humus decreases::decompos,humus,fall,fewer"),
S("State the difference between biotic and abiotic components",2,"Biotic components are the living things in an ecosystem; abiotic components are the non living things such as water, light, air, soil and temperature.",
  "Biotic components are the living things::biotic,living|Abiotic components are the non living things::abiotic,non living,non-living,nonliving"),
S("Name three abiotic components of an ecosystem",3,"Any three of: sunlight, temperature, water, air, soil, wind, rainfall.",
  "Sunlight::sunlight,light|Temperature::temperature|Water::water|Air::air,oxygen|Soil::soil|Wind or rainfall::wind,rain,rainfall"),
S("Give two examples of terrestrial ecosystems in Ghana",2,"Kakum forest, the savanna of Mole National Park, a farm, a school compound or a rocky hill.",
  "Forest such as Kakum::forest,kakum|Savanna grassland such as Mole::savanna,grassland,mole|Farm, garden or school compound::farm,garden,compound,hill"),
S("State the role of decomposers in an ecosystem",2,"Decomposers such as bacteria and fungi break down dead organisms and waste and return nutrients to the soil or water.",DEC),
S("State one adaptation of a fish for life in water",1,"A fish has gills for taking in dissolved oxygen, fins and a streamlined body for swimming.","Gills, fins or a streamlined body::gills,gill,fins,fin,streamlined,swim"),
S("Give one way in which plants affect the soil",1,"Plants shade the soil and lower its temperature, their roots hold soil together and reduce erosion, and fallen leaves decay into humus.","Shade the soil, roots hold soil, or leaves decay into humus::shade,roots,erosion,humus,decay,hold"),
S("Why do aquatic ecosystems have tiny floating plants near the surface",2,"Light reaches only the upper layer of the water, so the tiny floating plants (phytoplankton) grow near the surface where there is light to make food. They form the base of the food chain.",
  "Light reaches only the upper layer of the water::light,upper,surface|They make food and form the base of the food chain::food,base,food chain,photosynthesis"),
S("A farmer drains a small pond near his farm",2,"The water, a habitat and abiotic factor, is lost, so fish, tadpoles and water plants die or leave; the animals that fed on them lose their food.",
  "Organisms lose their water habitat and die or leave::die,leave,habitat,lose,water|Other animals that fed on them lose food, so the food chain is broken::food,feed,chain,predators,kingfisher"),
S("A community cuts down the trees along a stream",2,"Soil is washed into the stream (erosion) and the water warms up without shade; animals that depended on the trees lose food and shelter.",
  "Soil erosion or washing of soil into the stream::erosion,soil,washed,muddy|Less shade, so temperature rises, or animals lose food and shelter::shade,temperature,warm,food,shelter,habitat"),
S("Plastic waste is thrown into a lagoon every day",3,"Plastic blocks light, so producers make less food; consumers may eat or be trapped by plastic and die; and the water is polluted, lowering dissolved oxygen.",
  "Producers: plastic blocks light so less photosynthesis::light,producers,algae,photosynthesis|Consumers: animals eat or are trapped by plastic and may die::eat,trapped,die,consumers,fish|Abiotic: water is polluted or oxygen is reduced::polluted,pollution,oxygen,water"),
S("A school garden needs to be improved",2,"Decomposers break down dead plants and waste into humus and return nutrients to the soil, so the soil becomes richer and plants grow better.",DEC),
]
